caldun (1.7.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the MATE applet disappearing from the panel after a reboot. The factory
    callback was gated on "iid == 'CaldunApplet'" and returned False for
    anything else, building no widget at all. The panel does not pass the same
    iid string when restoring a session as when an applet is added
    interactively, so the applet worked when added and was gone after the next
    login, leaving a 1x1 placeholder. Returning False is not an error, so
    nothing was logged and no process crashed — the factory just sat idle.
    The callback now builds unconditionally, as every stock MATE applet
    (xapp, mintMenu, clock) does; this factory serves one applet type, so there
    is nothing to dispatch on. It also logs the iid it was given and prints a
    traceback instead of failing silently, so a future failure is visible in
    the journal.

 -- Oliver Glas <oliver.glas@inss.ch>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 17:05:00 +0200

caldun (1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a bug that suppressed genuine GPU overheat alarms on AMD systems. The
    sensor scan matched any "*_input" field, so amdgpu's voltage channels
    (vddgfx, ~0.7 V) and power channel were treated as temperature siblings.
    A 97°C GPU was therefore compared against a 0.7 "°C" sibling, tagged
    [SUSPECT] as a faulty reading, and reported as "All sensors within normal
    range" with exit code 0. Only temp*_input fields are read now. Any machine
    with an amdgpu, radeon or i915 chip was affected.
  * Add --json: the full report as one JSON object (schema 1), so consumers no
    longer scrape a formatted table whose layout can change. Includes per-sensor
    thresholds, their source, status, and the resolved model/firmware/defect/
    solution for suspect readings.
  * Add --get CATEGORY: print one bare number and nothing else, for scripts.
    Where a category holds several sensors the hottest wins; CATEGORY:CHIP
    selects one exactly. Exits 4 when the category has no sensor.
  * Add --watch [N]: sample every N seconds (default 30), one compact
    timestamped line per sample, or JSONL with --json. Prints a peak summary on
    exit. Notifications are rate limited to one per sensor per state change.
    Watch mode exits 0 on interrupt: its status reports that the run ended, not
    the last sample's temperature.
  * Add --peak and peak tracking. Every run, including the timer's, records a
    high-water mark per sensor under $XDG_STATE_HOME/caldun/peaks; peaks reset
    at reboot. SUSPECT readings are tracked separately and never become a
    sensor's peak, so a phantom spike cannot poison the record.
  * Report the CPU clock, and fan speeds where the machine has trustworthy ones.
    The clock is the mean across cores, not the maximum, because caldun is
    itself load on the busiest core. A percentage is shown only against a
    ceiling caldun trusts: cpuinfo_max_freq under amd_pstate/intel_pstate, and
    otherwise the highest clock ever observed on the machine — under
    acpi-cpufreq cpuinfo_max_freq is merely the base clock (1.8 GHz on a Ryzen 7
    4800U that boosts to 4.2 GHz), which would put idle above 100%. New
    settings SHOW_CLOCKS, SHOW_FANS, MIN_FREQ_SAMPLES, PEAK_TRACK.
  * Rewrite the argument parser so options can take values, and reject usage
    errors with exit code 64.
  * Add --version, and show the version in the --help header and in the --json
    output. Previously the version existed only in the package metadata, so an
    installed caldun could not say which release it was. debian/rules now
    refuses to build when the script's VERSION and debian/changelog disagree.
  * The MATE applet now consumes --json instead of scraping the text report, and
    shows suspect sensors in their own colour. It falls back to scraping when it
    finds a caldun older than 1.7, which happens on upgrade until the panel
    restarts.
  * Add CALDUN_SENSORS_JSON, which reads sensor data from a file instead of
    running sensors(1), so the tool can be exercised where the hardware is
    absent or the interesting state is rare.
  * Add GitHub Actions: build the .deb on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04 (the Mint 21
    and Mint 22 bases), shellcheck and man-page lint, a smoke test, and a
    tag-triggered release that refuses to publish when the tag and
    debian/changelog disagree.
  * Update the man page, which still documented the pre-rename "check-temps"
    command.

 -- Oliver Glas <oliver.glas@inss.ch>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 15:40:00 +0200

caldun (1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rename the project from check-temps to Caldun: command (/usr/bin/caldun),
    package, config (/etc/caldun.conf, /etc/caldun-known-issues.conf), systemd
    user units, MATE panel applet and D-Bus factory, icon, and man page. This is
    a clean break with no compatibility shims for the old name.
  * Add faulty-sensor detection. A reading that alarms while every other channel
    on the same chip stays below WARN (and more than ANOMALY_MARGIN cooler) is
    flagged [SUSPECT] and reported separately instead of raising a false thermal
    alarm; a genuine overheat (all channels hot) still alerts as before. New
    settings ANOMALY_DETECT (default on) and ANOMALY_MARGIN in
    /etc/caldun.conf.
  * Add a known-issues database (/etc/caldun-known-issues.conf) mapping a
    device model/firmware to a defect description and a solution, shown (and sent
    as a separate notification) when a SUSPECT reading matches.
  * Ship a default entry for the Samsung SSD 980 firmware 2B4QFXO7 bug, which
    intermittently reports phantom ~84°C "Composite" spikes while the drive's
    real sensors stay ~60°C; the fix is a firmware update to 3B4QFXO7 or later.

 -- Oliver Glas <oliver.glas@inss.ch>  Tue, 30 Jun 2026 10:30:00 +0200

caldun (1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add "temps" command (/usr/bin/temps): runs caldun and shows the
    report in a desktop notification, for triggering the popup manually
    (panel launcher, keyboard shortcut) without a shell alias. Notification
    urgency follows the caldun exit code (OK/WARN/CRITICAL).
  * Promote libnotify-bin from Recommends to Depends: the new "temps" command
    relies on notify-send unconditionally, so it must always be present.

 -- Oliver Glas <oliver.glas@inss.ch>  Tue, 24 Jun 2026 12:45:00 +0200

caldun (1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix applet not appearing in the MATE panel after "Add to Panel". The D-Bus
    activation service was named org.mate.panel.applet.CaldunFactory, but
    the panel derives the bus name from the factory Id (CaldunAppletFactory)
    and so requested org.mate.panel.applet.CaldunAppletFactory at startup —
    a name D-Bus could not activate, so the panel silently skipped the factory.
    Renamed the service to match the factory Id, per the MATE convention used by
    all stock applets. Manual launch had masked this by self-registering the
    correct bus name.
  * Remove temporary debug syslog logging from the applet fill callback.

 -- Oliver Glas <oliver.glas@inss.ch>  Wed, 24 Jun 2026 11:15:00 +0200

caldun (1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Add MATE panel applet (caldun-applet) for live temperature display
    on Ubuntu and Linux Mint with the MATE desktop.
  * Applet shows CPU, GPU, and drive temperatures colour-coded by status;
    left-click to force refresh; tooltip shows full caldun report.
    Registers via D-Bus so MATE panel discovers it automatically.
  * Ship custom thermometer+circuit icon (caldun) in hicolor theme at
    16, 22, 24, 32, 48, 256, and 512 px; used in the "Add to Panel" dialog.
  * Add to Recommends: python3-gi, gir1.2-gtk-3.0, gir1.2-matepanelapplet-4.0.

